From 41c82bed578e52f701ba0049e46a3e612bca2737 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: QC3284 <2078725082@qq.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 14:45:54 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] spi: add the ZX279128S SPI flash controller

Add the SPI flash controller driver for the ZTE/Sanechips ZX279128S
SoC and wire it into the A1/A2 device tree, so the custom U-Boot can
probe the SPI-NAND and read it with the built-in mtd command.

The driver is a U-Boot port of the PIO path of the vendor kernel
driver that ships with the E2631 kernel tree. The register layout,
the FIFO protocol and the exec_op sequence follow that driver, which
in turn carries ZTE's SDK register definitions. DMA, interrupts,
reset control and clock setup are left out on purpose: secondboot has
already brought the controller up and the firmware only needs to read
a few KiB of flash in the RAM chain. The clock is optional - when the
device tree has no clock provider the driver keeps the rate secondboot
programmed, which is also why the new node has no "clocks" property.

U-Boot's spi-mem core drives the controller through exec_op and falls
back to spi_mem_no_dirmap_read() when there is no dirmap, so the
driver only implements mem_ops.exec_op, supports_op and adjust_op_size.
Data is moved through the 32-bit PIO FIFO; unlike the kernel driver
the byte count is not rounded to whole words, so the one-byte
SET_FEATURE transfers used during SPI-NAND probing do not read or
write past their buffers.

Enable SPI, MTD and SPI-NAND in the defconfig and describe the
controller and the FORESEE SPI-NAND in the device tree. The chip
entry already exists in U-Boot (F35SQA001G, 0x71/0x71, 2048+64,
on-die ECC); the A2 stage only needs the read path, writes stay
unused until the slot boot work.

Signed-off-by: QC3284 <2078725082@qq.com>
---
 arch/arm/dts/zx279128s-zte-zxhn-e2631.dts  |  27 +-
 configs/zx279128s_zte_zxhn-e2631_defconfig |  11 +-
 drivers/spi/Kconfig                        |   9 +
 drivers/spi/Makefile                       |   1 +
 drivers/spi/spi-zx.c                       | 411 +++++++++++++++++++++
 5 files changed, 454 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 drivers/spi/spi-zx.c

diff --git a/arch/arm/dts/zx279128s-zte-zxhn-e2631.dts b/arch/arm/dts/zx279128s-zte-zxhn-e2631.dts
index b7c2d4b6..ba395dc8 100644
--- a/arch/arm/dts/zx279128s-zte-zxhn-e2631.dts
+++ b/arch/arm/dts/zx279128s-zte-zxhn-e2631.dts
@@ -2,9 +2,9 @@
 /*
  * ZTE ZXHN E2631 (Sanechips ZX279128S), A1 RAM-chain device tree
  *
- * Only the pieces the A1 port uses are described: DRAM, the PL011 console
- * and the Cortex-A9 global timer. SPI-NAND, Ethernet and CPU1 hand-off are
- * intentionally left out until the A2 stage.
+ * Only the pieces the A1/A2 port uses are described: DRAM, the PL011 console,
+ * the Cortex-A9 global timer and the SPI-NAND controller. Ethernet and the
+ * CPU1 hand-off are intentionally left out until a later stage.
  */
 
 /dts-v1/;
@@ -55,4 +55,25 @@
 		compatible = "zte,zx29-uart";
 		reg = <0x94404000 0x1000>;
 	};
+
+	/*
+	 * SPI-NAND controller. secondboot already brought it up, U-Boot only
+	 * needs the PIO read path. There is deliberately no "clocks" property:
+	 * U-Boot has no ZX CRPM clock driver, so the driver keeps the rate
+	 * secondboot programmed. See uboot-port/a2-spi-nand-design.md.
+	 */
+	spi@94406000 {
+		compatible = "zte,zx279128s-spifc";
+		reg = <0x94406000 0x1000>;
+		#address-cells = <1>;
+		#size-cells = <0>;
+
+		flash@0 {
+			compatible = "spi-nand";
+			reg = <0>;
+			spi-max-frequency = <50000000>;
+			spi-rx-bus-width = <2>;
+			spi-tx-bus-width = <1>;
+		};
+	};
 };
